Packing in C/C++
Structure padding is the compiler-inserted unused space between or after members of a structure to satisfy alignment requirements.
Alignment improves memory access efficiency and is defined by the platform ABI.  

Why Padding Exists
Modern CPUs access naturally aligned data more efficiently. An int is typically aligned to a 4-byte boundary and a double to an 8-byte boundary. 
If a member is misaligned, some processors require multiple memory accesses while others may even raise an alignment fault.

Example 1
struct test { char a; int b; }
Naive layout: |a|b|b|b|b| (5 bytes) Actual layout: |a|-|-|-|b|b|b|b| (8 bytes) The compiler inserts 3 bytes of padding so that 'int b' starts at offset 4.

Example 2
struct test1 { char a; short b; } Layout: |a|-|b|b|  -> sizeof = 4

Example 3
struct test2 { char a; double b; } Layout: |a|-------padding-------|double| sizeof = 16

Tail Padding
The compiler may also insert padding at the end of a structure so that every element in an array begins at the required alignment boundary.
Packing

#pragma pack(1) or __attribute__((packed)) removes compiler-inserted padding. Packed structures save memory but may cause slower accesses or alignment faults on some architectures.

When to Use Packing
  Network protocol headers
  Binary file formats
  Memory-mapped hardware registers
  Memory-constrained embedded systems
  Structure Reordering
  Reordering members from largest alignment to smallest often reduces padding and memory usage.
  Compiler / ABI Dependence

Structure layout depends on the ABI. For example, sizeof(long) is 8 bytes on most Linux/macOS 64-bit systems but 4 bytes on Windows 64-bit.

Best Practices
Prefer natural alignment unless exact binary layout is required.
Use #pragma pack only around specific structures.
Packed structures should not be the default for application code.
Always verify layouts using sizeof() and offsetof().
